# Swift/iOS patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
Swift concurrency and SwiftUI state ownership. Applies only to `*.swift`
files.

## Swift concurrency: async/await and structured concurrency

- Prefer `async`/`await` over completion-handler callbacks for new
  asynchronous code — it reads linearly and composes with structured
  concurrency instead of nesting closures.
- Start concurrent child work with `async let` (a small, fixed number of
  parallel tasks) or `TaskGroup`/`withThrowingTaskGroup` (a dynamic or
  unbounded set of child tasks); both guarantee every child task is
  awaited or cancelled before the parent scope exits, unlike a detached
  `Task {}` with no owner.
- Reach for a bare `Task {}` only at a genuine "fire from non-async
  context" boundary (a SwiftUI `.task` modifier, a button action); give
  it an explicit reason it does not need to be joined, and prefer
  `.task { }` on a SwiftUI view over a manually created `Task` when the
  work should be tied to the view's lifetime (it is cancelled
  automatically when the view disappears).
- Respect cancellation in long-running async work: check
  `Task.isCancelled`/call `try Task.checkCancellation()` in a loop, so a
  cancelled parent actually stops the child's work instead of running to
  completion regardless.

## Actors and @MainActor

- Use `actor` to protect mutable state that multiple tasks touch
  concurrently — its methods are implicitly isolated, so the compiler
  (not a convention) prevents unsynchronized access, the same guarantee
  a manual lock only provides by discipline.
- Mark any type whose state SwiftUI reads or mutates `@MainActor` — view
  models, `@Observable` stores, anything a `View`'s `body` touches —
  since UIKit/SwiftUI rendering must happen on the main actor. Isolate at
  the type level (`@MainActor final class ...`) rather than sprinkling
  `@MainActor` on individual methods, unless only some of the type's
  methods genuinely need main-actor isolation.
- Cross an actor boundary explicitly with `await`; do not reach for
  `@unchecked Sendable` or `nonisolated(unsafe)` to make a boundary
  error disappear — either the type's state genuinely needs no
  isolation (make that case explicitly, with a comment), or it needs a
  real actor/lock, not a suppression.

## Sendable conformance

- A value crossing a concurrency boundary (passed into a `Task`, an
  actor method, or a `TaskGroup` child) must be `Sendable`: a `struct`
  or `enum` with only `Sendable` members is `Sendable` for free; a
  `final class` with only immutable (`let`) stored properties can
  declare conformance explicitly.
- Do not slap `@unchecked Sendable` on a class to silence a
  strict-concurrency diagnostic without actually auditing and
  documenting why its mutable state cannot race — that trades a
  compile-time guarantee for an unchecked promise, which is exactly the
  class of bug Swift 6's data-race safety exists to catch.
- Under the Swift 6 language mode, treat a "Sendable" or "actor
  isolation" compiler error as a real potential data race to fix at the
  root (add an actor, make the type immutable, or copy the value before
  crossing), not friction to suppress.

## SwiftUI state ownership

- `@State` owns a value the view itself creates and is the sole owner
  of — a simple value type, or (iOS 17+) an `@Observable` reference type
  the view instantiates directly (`@State private var model = Model()`,
  not `@StateObject` for `@Observable` types).
- `@Binding` is for a child view that needs to both read and mutate a
  value it does not own — the parent passes `$value`, and the child
  writes through the binding instead of receiving a copy it cannot
  propagate changes from.
- `@Environment` is for values that are ambient to a whole subtree
  (theme, a shared service, a feature flag) rather than threaded
  explicitly through every initializer; do not reach for it as a
  shortcut to avoid passing an explicit dependency that only one or two
  views actually need.
- A view that receives an `@Observable` model from a parent and needs to
  pass a binding to one of its properties down further uses `@Bindable`,
  not a manually reconstructed `Binding(get:set:)`.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A retain cycle from a closure (completion handler, Combine `sink`,
  stored callback) that captures `self` strongly when the closure
  outlives the call that created it — use `[weak self]` and unwrap, or
  `[unowned self]` only when `self`'s lifetime is provably guaranteed to
  exceed the closure's.
- Lifting a value into `@State`/`@StateObject` at the view that merely
  displays it, when a parent further up actually owns and mutates it —
  that duplicates state instead of using `@Binding`/`@Observable`
  references, and the two copies drift.
- A massive view or view-model type that owns navigation, networking,
  and persistence together — split by responsibility the same way a
  "god package"/"god object" is flagged in any other stack.
